  "ADR-2608160100 established block -> pack -> object as the physical plane.
This records what it took to WIRE it into a Worker that already has a
history, and three decisions that ADR did not contain.

## 1. The bootstrap circularity, and why the pointer needs no signature

Blocks live at `blocks/<graph>/<cid>`; the head record carries only
`{:chain-cid :sequence}`. Packing moves location to (pack CID, offset,
length), which the catalog answers -- so a catalog kept as datoms IN the
graph cannot be read without first reading the graph.

The pointer must therefore live outside the packs. Resolved in two parts:

- Each pack is sealed with the PREVIOUS pack's CID as its CARv2 root, so the
  packs form a chain. One CID reaches every earlier pack, and each pack's own
  CARv2 index locates its blocks. `bootstrap-catalog!` is that walk -- the
  catalog with the catalog taken away, which is what makes ADR-2608160100's
  word `projection` true rather than aspirational.
- The tip of that chain is one object beside the head (`packs/<graph>/
  tip.json`), UNSIGNED and deliberately not a field in the signed IPNS
  record.

The signature is unnecessary because every block leaving a pack is rehashed
against the CID that was asked for. A tip that is stale, missing, or planted
by whoever can write the bucket costs a lookup that FAILS; it cannot produce
a block that is wrong. What a bad tip can do is hide newer blocks, which is
the freshness problem a stale head already has. Integrity is the CID's job
here; the pointer is availability.

A pack cannot link to itself -- its CID is the hash of its own bytes -- so
the only constructible cycle involves packs written by something other than
this store, which is what the `seen` set is for. `max-packs` bounds a chain
that is merely very long. Two hazards, two mechanisms.

## 2. Reads before writes, and the order does not commute

Production writes through one call (`store/flush!`) and reads through
`fetch-block-cached`, which was L1 -> L2 -> one B2 object per CID. Switching
writes to packs while reads only know objects produces a graph whose new
blocks cannot be read back -- in an isolated environment too, just privately.

So the packed tier went in FRONT of the durable read first. It is safe to
land before anything writes packs because a graph with no packs has no tip,
an empty location table, and every CID disclaimed -- the read takes exactly
the path it took before. That is measured, not argued: all 233 pre-existing
engine tests pass unchanged AND still pass when the packed tier is broken.

Cost of wiring early: one `tip.json` 404 per graph per isolate, held for the
isolate's life. Stated in the docstring and pinned by a test, because a
round trip nobody can explain later is worse than one that is written down.

## 3. Removing the migration fallback is a measurement, not a date

`pack-migration` reads packed-first and sends only CIDs the catalog
DISCLAIMS to the per-object plane. It will not fall back for a CID the
catalog claims: if the catalog says (pack, offset, length) and the pack
cannot produce it, that is a storage fault, and serving the same CID from
the old plane would convert it into a silent success that stays in place
until the fallback is removed -- at which point it becomes data loss with
no warning in between.

`:fallback-lookups` is the stopping condition. While it is non-zero there is
history outside the packs and removing the fallback loses it. The counter is
tied to bytes actually served, not to how CIDs were classified: the first
version asserted only the classification and read 3 even with the fallback
deleted entirely.

## Current state (2026-08-17)

Landed on default branches, west pins advanced (root f78d1f7):

  net-kotobase/control-plane      e3dfb02  signed half-open ranged reads
  kotoba-lang/kotobase-storage    1b84313  contract that compiles in cljs
  kotoba-lang/kotobase-storage-pack f6a4da8 pack chain, async datom catalog
                                           (last 2 commits by another session)
  net-kotobase/engine             dd6a043  tip, migration, bootstrap catalog,
                                           read wiring, write gate, counters

PRODUCTION IS UNCHANGED. `KOTOBASE_PACK_WRITES` is off, so `store/flush!`
still PUTs one object per block and the packed tier finds nothing. The only
new cost is the tip 404 above.

## Open, in order

1. testnet (`kotobase-cf-wasm-testnet`, its own B2 bucket AND prefix) with
   KOTOBASE_PACK_WRITES=1 and KOTOBASE_TIMING=1. `wrangler tail` then shows
   `:packed-write` (what a commit became) and `:packed-reads` (what reading
   it cost). No measurement of the real thing exists yet -- every number so
   far is from the suites.
2. The production catalog. `pack-catalog-async/datom-catalog` needs
   `{:transact! :q}` against the engine's datom plane, which is unwired and
   is its own decision. `pack-catalog-bootstrap` is the bridge and its price
   is honest: it walks EVERY pack, so a thousand commits is a thousand
   object GETs per isolate, and a cold isolate pays for the whole history
   before returning one block.
3. Production cutover, gated on (1) and on `:fallback-lookups` reaching zero.

## Two upstream defects found by building this

- `kotobase-storage/validate-object-store!` passed a computed protocol to
  `satisfies?`, which is a function on the JVM and under nbb but a
  compile-time macro in ClojureScript. The namespace did not build with the
  compiler that builds the Workers. Both CI gates are JVM or nbb, so neither
  could catch it: an interpreter never performs the macroexpansion that
  fails. Fixed in 1b84313; `clojure -M:cljs-compile` added there as the
  missing question.
- `net-kotobase/engine` could not `npm run test:cljs` from a clean checkout:
  a `file:` dependency pointed at a sibling path that exists in no layout.
  deps.edn carried a comment about having fixed exactly this for the clojure
  dep; the npm side was left behind."}]
